# The crew slug that OWNS the health domain. This is a declared ROLE, not an
# instance identity -- which is exactly why it is named here once instead of
# repeating the historical health-agent slug at six write sites.
#
# HISTORICAL ROLE VALUE UNTIL 2026-09-24. The value transcribed a
# standing product rule, not an accident: 2026-08-20, meal belongs to the
# health domain, so owning_agent stays the health agent's slug; restated 2026-09-08
# (both agents may carry the skill, the health agent owns it) when 2.19.0 split diet-log
# between the kit's capture skill and pack_health's diet-status. The capture
# surface is provenance; the DOMAIN has one owner and the row carries it. Six
# call sites used to each carry the bare literal with the reasoning written out
# at only one of them, which is how a ruling turns into a hardcode: nobody can
# re-rule it without finding all six, and nobody can tell a deliberate role slug
# apart from a stale copy-paste by looking.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT. It is NOT the pre-049 schema hardcode, which was a different
# defect with a different repair: the twelve ax_*/goal_*/metric_*/hypothesis/
# pain_event tables CONSTRAINED owning_agent to a closed list of historical slugs and so
# REFUSED a newly minted agent's own slug outright -- fixed by migration
# 049_owning_agent_identity.sql, not by this constant. The tables this constant
# stamps (meals / workouts / metric_log / routine_def) carry no such CHECK; the
# value there is a label, and the label is the owner's to set.
#
# WHO OWNS IT NOW (2.27.0; re-ruled 2026-09-24). Two owner rulings the same
# morning: 08:31 "the owner is 'ag' now; it moves to the health agent when that
# agent is minted, past rows included", and 10:17 "if a hand is needed,
# dogfooding failed". So the owner is DERIVED from facts the machine already
# holds, and past rows follow it without anyone running a verb. Resolution
# order, first hit wins:
#   1. env LIFEKIT_HEALTH_AGENT     -- per-process override (tests, a second
#                                     health instance on purpose).
#   2. crew override                -- config row HEALTH_DOMAIN_AGENT_KEY in the
#                                     crew-shared kit_life.db, written only by
#                                     `health-agent-set`. A manual OVERRIDE for
#                                     a crew whose facts point the wrong way;
#                                     the normal path never writes it.
#   3. derived                      -- the crew registry (crew.conf beside the
#                                     crew DB; an unlinked DB = a solo instance)
#                                     and each member's .instance.conf: the ONE
#                                     member carrying pack_health, else the ONE
#                                     main-class member. _health_agent_derived.
#   4. _HEALTH_DOMAIN_AGENT_DEFAULT -- no fact decides.
# WHY THE CREW DB FOR THE OVERRIDE, NOT AN ENV VAR OR crew.conf. An env var is
# per-process: bridge-spawned turns and launchd routines do not share an
# environment, so an env-only ruling stamps two different owners depending on
# which process wrote the row. The crew DB is the one object every process of
# every member already opens. crew.conf is READ here (member= lines only) but
# never written: the framework readers of it (crew_consent / crew_lease /
# crew_leave) fail closed on any line that is not member=.
# HANDOVER. The crew DB also records the last crew-wide owner a health write
# stamped (HEALTH_DOMAIN_OWNER_RECORD_KEY, kit-internal, distinct from the
# override row). A health-domain write that resolves a different owner relabels
# the recorded owner's health rows to the new one inside that write's own
